 "rulebook": "World Athletics Competition and Technical Rules, 2024 edition: TR 31 (31.1 the hop, step and jump in that order; 31.2 the hop lands on the take-off foot, the step on the other foot; the rules of the long jump apply: TR 29 the runway, the board, the landing area; TR 30.1.1, 30.1.2, 30.1.5 failures; TR 30.3 the measurement), TR 25.17 (one minute for a trial)",
 "deviations": [
  "TR 31.3 (the take-off line at least 13 m from the pit for men, 11 m for women in international competition; nearer boards for other levels): the board is 7.0 m before the pit, a school competition's board (FAMILIES.md)",
  "TR 31.2, note (a touch of the ground by the 'sleeping' leg is no failure): not applied: between the hop's landing and the step's take-off, and between the step's landing and the jump's take-off, only the foot the phase landed on may touch the ground; a referee without eyes cannot tell a scrape from a supporting step",
  "TR 31.1 (the event is one hop, one step and one jump): between a phase's landing and the next take-off the landing foot is on one place: a touch of it more than 0.3 m from where it landed is a failure (hops too short to be flights, or a slide, would otherwise cover the distance)",
  "the take-off of the hop from one foot (implied by TR 31.2) is judged by a number: the other foot must have left the ground at least 0.05 s before the take-off foot does",
  "TR 30.1.3 (somersaulting) and TR 30.1.6 / 30.2 (how the athlete leaves the landing area) are not judged; the landing is judged for 2.0 s from the first contact with the sand",
  "TR 29.3 (the plasticine indicator board): no imprint; the take-off line is judged geometrically: a foot on the ground with any part of it (also a part in the air) on or over the line's plane is a failure",
  "TR 29.6 (the sand): the pit is a box with a soft, overdamped, high-friction contact (time constant 0.05 s, full stiffness only 0.04 m deep, friction 1.5); it keeps no imprint, so the mark is the contact with the sand nearest the take-off line",
  "TR 31 does not define a hop; here each phase is a flight of at least 0.05 s, and the hop must raise the pelvis at least 0.05 m above its height at lift-off; no wind; one trial"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Do a triple jump: run up, hop from behind the take-off line and land on the same foot, step onto the other foot, and jump from it into the sand pit.",
  "rules": "World Athletics rules for the triple jump. The runway is 40.0 m long and 1.22 m wide along +x and goes on to the pit. The take-off line is x = 0, the edge of the white take-off board (0.2 m long) that is nearer the pit. The landing area is a pit of sand, level with the runway, that begins 7.0 m beyond the take-off line and is 9.0 m long and 2.75 m wide, centred on y = 0. A flight is a time of at least 0.05 s in which nothing of you touches anything. Until the hop: no foot that is on the ground may have any part of it on or over the take-off line's plane (the whole foot counts, also the parts of it that are in the air), and nothing of you may touch the ground at x >= 0. (1) The hop is the flight that ends with your first contact beyond the line. It must be taken off from one foot within the runway's width: the other foot has left the ground at least 0.05 s before the take-off foot; your pelvis must rise at least 0.05 m above its height at lift-off; and its first contact must be the take-off foot alone, on the runway. (2) The step is the next flight: until it starts only the foot you landed on may touch the ground, and its first contact must be the other foot alone, on the runway. (3) The jump is the next flight: until it starts only the foot the step landed on may touch the ground, and its first contact must be with the sand (any part of your body). Between the landing of the hop or of the step and the next flight the landing foot stays where it came down: it fails to touch the ground with its centre more than 0.3 m from where it was at the landing (no sliding along and no travelling in hops shorter than a flight). On the runway means: every point of that contact within the runway's width and before the pit. The mark is the point nearest the take-off line at which any part of you touches the sand in the first 2.0 s after the jump's first contact; in that time you may not touch the ground outside the pit nearer the take-off line than the mark. You succeed when that time has passed after a valid hop, step and jump: the mark is then at least 7.0 m. A fall is not a failure by itself. You fail on any of the faults above, if you leave the venue (the level ground ends 8 m to each side of the runway's axis, 45 m behind the take-off line and 6 m beyond the pit) or after 60 s (the time allowed for a trial).",
  "venue": "You stand on the runway facing the pit (+x), your pelvis 20 m before the take-off line, in the middle of the runway; the runway goes on 20 m behind you. The runway is red with white edge lines, the board white, the sand yellow, the ground around it grass. Everything is level.",
  "difficulty": "Seven metres in three flights, each from one foot: every phase has to be more than twice this robot's best published two-foot jump, taken from a single leg, and landed on a single leg with enough balance and speed left to take off again. Nothing built in hops on one foot at all."
 },
